  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows mid/upper troughing across the\nNorthern Plains and encroaching on the Upper Midwest, with a \nband of 40+ knot H5 winds extending from northern Colorado into \nNE Minnesota and western Ontario. At the surface, a cold front\nis analyzed northeast to southwest through Iowa, into extreme \nSE Nebraska and further into SW Kansas, where a weak surface\ncyclone has developed. At this time, showers and a few \nthunderstorms are ongoing, primarily north of the cold front \n(elevated), across portions of eastern Nebraska and western \nIowa. Meanwhile, south of the front across our CWA, temperatures\nhave risen into the upper 80s as of 2 PM, with afternoon Max Ts\nexpected to reach the lower to even mid 90s (south of the KC \nmetro) by later this afternoon. The front is likely to stall out\nand become essentially stationary by later this\nafternoon/evening across NW Missouri into NE Kansas. While\nstronger mid level flow and better forcing for ascent should\nremain north of the front, cannot completely rule out a strong\nthunderstorm or two late afternoon into early evening toward the\nIowa border thanks to a moderately unstable air mass in place\nalong and south of the front, which has prompted SPC to include\nnorthern Missouri within a marginal risk for severe storms. \nHowever, CAM guidance has trended toward keeping convection \nprimarily north of the Iowa border. By late tonight into the \novernight period. a vort max currently over Colorado, pushes \neastward toward our region, and along with a modest low level \njet, should help develop showers and thunderstorms north of the \nfront across SE Nebraska into SW Iowa. Once again, most of this \nactivity should remain north of our CWA, but may sneak southward\ninto northern Missouri. \n\nAs we head into tomorrow, another mid level vort maxima moves \ninto the region as broad mid/upper troughing across the north\ncentral CONUS digs a little further south into the region, with \nthe stalled front still in place across the CWA, which should \nhelp generate isolated to scattered showers and storms tomorrow \nmorning into early afternoon, with another round of convection \npossible late tomorrow evening into early Thursday morning. With\ndew points in the upper 60s to lower 70s, decent mid level flow\nallowing for 30 to 35 knots of effective shear, and 1500 to \n2000 J/kg of ML CAPE, a few storms could be strong to severe. \nAdditionally, with PWATs in the 1.8\" to 2\" range and deep warm \ncloud depths, locally moderate to heavy rainfall may be \npossible. There will be yet another chance for showers and \nthunderstorms late Thursday into Friday morning. \n\nBy Friday and into the weekend, the western ridge re-establishes\nitself, with a 597 dam H5 high developing over the Four Corners\nregion, yielding increasing mid level heights over our CWA. \nAfternoon highs should return to the 90s across the CWA for \nSaturday and Sunday, with max heat indices ranging from the \nupper 90s to the mid 100s, with generally dry weather likely.",
